WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.480
Automating an Android release is relatively straightforward,

2
00:00:03.480 --> 00:00:06.280
but deploying an iOS application requires

3
00:00:06.280 --> 00:00:09.080
penetrating Apple's tightly sealed ecosystem.

4
00:00:09.080 --> 00:00:12.600
You cannot compile an IPA file on Windows or Linux.

5
00:00:12.600 --> 00:00:15.560
The process strictly dictates Mac OS hardware,

6
00:00:15.560 --> 00:00:18.640
Xcode software, and Apple provision certificates.

7
00:00:18.640 --> 00:00:20.640
This creates a massive dilemma for

8
00:00:20.640 --> 00:00:22.240
engineering teams trying to automate

9
00:00:22.240 --> 00:00:24.880
their continuous integration and deployment pipelines.

10
00:00:24.880 --> 00:00:26.360
You have two options.

11
00:00:26.360 --> 00:00:28.720
Option A is fighting through the configuration

12
00:00:28.720 --> 00:00:31.360
friction of GitHub actions to build a unified pipeline

13
00:00:31.360 --> 00:00:34.480
that handles both iOS and Android side-by-side.

14
00:00:34.480 --> 00:00:37.240
Option B is accepting complete ecosystem lock-in

15
00:00:37.240 --> 00:00:39.880
with Xcode Cloud, trading platform independence

16
00:00:39.880 --> 00:00:42.240
for zero configuration simplicity.

17
00:00:42.240 --> 00:00:44.640
This flow chart maps a single code commit.

18
00:00:44.640 --> 00:00:47.240
It splits into two paths across platform route,

19
00:00:47.240 --> 00:00:50.200
carrying configuration warnings, and a streamlined Apple-only

20
00:00:50.200 --> 00:00:50.960
route.

21
00:00:50.960 --> 00:00:53.520
Routing through GitHub actions requires

22
00:00:53.520 --> 00:00:57.720
Cloud-hosted Mac OS runners, which cost roughly 10 times more

23
00:00:57.720 --> 00:01:00.160
per minute than standard Linux runners.

24
00:01:00.160 --> 00:01:03.600
Without precise optimization, a standard iOS build

25
00:01:03.600 --> 00:01:06.200
pulls gigabytes of native dependencies.

26
00:01:06.200 --> 00:01:08.160
Developers are routinely left waiting

27
00:01:08.160 --> 00:01:10.840
30 minutes to see if they're code compiles.

28
00:01:10.840 --> 00:01:15.080
When you multiply a 30-minute wait time by a 10-X cost multiplier

29
00:01:15.080 --> 00:01:18.080
for every single commit, an unmanaged pipeline

30
00:01:18.080 --> 00:01:20.480
will silently drain your CI budget.

31
00:01:20.480 --> 00:01:23.520
Your choice of pipeline functions as a critical financial

32
00:01:23.520 --> 00:01:26.360
and operational investment dictated entirely

33
00:01:26.400 --> 00:01:28.120
by how your team is structured.

34
00:01:28.120 --> 00:01:30.200
The first major hurdle with GitHub actions

35
00:01:30.200 --> 00:01:32.520
is exactly that hardware requirement.

36
00:01:32.520 --> 00:01:36.280
You are relying on expensive Cloud-hosted Mac OS runners.

37
00:01:36.280 --> 00:01:38.640
You need them because they provide immediate access

38
00:01:38.640 --> 00:01:41.920
to exclusive build tools like Xcode Build and XC Run,

39
00:01:41.920 --> 00:01:44.640
which are impossible to replicate on Linux.

40
00:01:44.640 --> 00:01:47.280
But relying on the default Mac OS latest tag

41
00:01:47.280 --> 00:01:48.240
is a risk.

42
00:01:48.240 --> 00:01:51.240
When GitHub automatically updates the runner environment,

43
00:01:51.240 --> 00:01:54.520
an unpinned exclude version will suddenly break your builds

44
00:01:54.520 --> 00:01:56.560
due to subtle compiler changes.

45
00:01:56.560 --> 00:01:59.160
To defend against that 10-X cost multiplier,

46
00:01:59.160 --> 00:02:02.200
your primary operational defense is aggressive dependency

47
00:02:02.200 --> 00:02:03.040
caching.

48
00:02:03.040 --> 00:02:05.680
You have to cache your node modules and pre-built Hermes

49
00:02:05.680 --> 00:02:06.640
binaries.

50
00:02:06.640 --> 00:02:09.560
By using the lock file hashes as your invalidation keys,

51
00:02:09.560 --> 00:02:11.440
you ensure the runner only downloads them

52
00:02:11.440 --> 00:02:13.320
when versions actually change.

53
00:02:13.320 --> 00:02:17.000
This two-by-two matrix charts set up effort against cost-saved,

54
00:02:17.000 --> 00:02:19.440
implementing CocoaPod spec caching lands firmly

55
00:02:19.440 --> 00:02:21.760
in the low effort high-reward quadrant.

56
00:02:21.760 --> 00:02:23.720
Execution must be precise.

57
00:02:23.720 --> 00:02:25.840
The key is separating the rarely changing spec

58
00:02:25.840 --> 00:02:28.640
repository from the actual compiled dependencies.

59
00:02:28.640 --> 00:02:30.280
If you merge them into a single cache,

60
00:02:30.280 --> 00:02:33.640
your project will hoard 500 megabytes or more of dependencies,

61
00:02:33.640 --> 00:02:36.360
leading to severe storage bloat and painfully slow cache

62
00:02:36.360 --> 00:02:37.440
restoration.

63
00:02:37.440 --> 00:02:40.040
When executed correctly, pinning the runner

64
00:02:40.040 --> 00:02:42.200
and separating the dependency caches

65
00:02:42.200 --> 00:02:46.920
cuts a heavy 30-minute build down to a tight 8 to 12 minutes.

66
00:02:46.920 --> 00:02:49.080
On GitHub actions, aggressive caching

67
00:02:49.080 --> 00:02:52.560
serves as a mandatory requirement for financial survival,

68
00:02:52.560 --> 00:02:56.200
keeping Mac OS runner casts from spiraling out of control.

69
00:02:56.200 --> 00:02:58.040
Once the build speeds are under control,

70
00:02:58.040 --> 00:03:00.680
you hit the most complex obstacle in the pipeline,

71
00:03:00.680 --> 00:03:02.400
Apple code signing.

72
00:03:02.400 --> 00:03:04.280
You can manually import certificates

73
00:03:04.280 --> 00:03:07.520
and provisioning profiles directly via GitHub secrets.

74
00:03:07.520 --> 00:03:10.280
It works for a solo project, but it falls apart the moment

75
00:03:10.280 --> 00:03:13.680
a growing team needs to rotate a key or add a device.

76
00:03:13.680 --> 00:03:16.000
The scalable alternative is Fastlane Match.

77
00:03:16.000 --> 00:03:18.240
It automates the entire process of generating

78
00:03:18.240 --> 00:03:20.160
and deploying certificates.

79
00:03:20.160 --> 00:03:22.080
Looking back at our two-by-two matrix,

80
00:03:22.080 --> 00:03:24.920
adding Fastlane Match plots high on the reward scale,

81
00:03:24.920 --> 00:03:27.480
but it carries a massive initial setup effort.

82
00:03:27.480 --> 00:03:30.600
You have to configure encrypted passwords, supply,

83
00:03:30.600 --> 00:03:34.880
app store, connect API keys, and maintain a dedicated private

84
00:03:34.880 --> 00:03:38.440
Git repository just to hold the encrypted certificates.

85
00:03:38.440 --> 00:03:41.960
It is frustrating to set up, but the operational outcome

86
00:03:41.960 --> 00:03:43.960
is perfectly synchronized certificates

87
00:03:43.960 --> 00:03:47.800
across your entire engineering team and your CI pipeline,

88
00:03:47.800 --> 00:03:49.480
pushing through the friction of match

89
00:03:49.480 --> 00:03:52.440
and strict caching, earns you the ultimate reward,

90
00:03:52.440 --> 00:03:55.280
parallel execution inside a mono repo.

91
00:03:55.280 --> 00:03:57.760
This split-screen flowcharts and Android build

92
00:03:57.760 --> 00:04:00.400
running on a Linux node and an iOS build

93
00:04:00.400 --> 00:04:02.160
running on a Mac OS node.

94
00:04:02.160 --> 00:04:04.640
Because they share one central repository,

95
00:04:04.640 --> 00:04:07.800
GitHub actions triggers them at the exact same time.

96
00:04:07.800 --> 00:04:10.200
The iOS job doesn't wait for Android.

97
00:04:10.200 --> 00:04:13.000
The pipelines run completely independent of each other,

98
00:04:13.000 --> 00:04:14.680
maximizing your deployment speed

99
00:04:14.680 --> 00:04:17.280
while keeping all your platform code unified.

100
00:04:17.280 --> 00:04:19.460
Side-by-side execution also exposes

101
00:04:19.460 --> 00:04:21.500
the exact financial trade-off.

102
00:04:21.500 --> 00:04:23.700
The Linux runner finishes the Android build

103
00:04:23.700 --> 00:04:26.060
quickly and cheaply, while the Mac OS runner

104
00:04:26.060 --> 00:04:29.060
grinds through the iOS build at a premium rate.

105
00:04:29.060 --> 00:04:32.260
Enduring the friction of Fastlane and the complexity of caching

106
00:04:32.260 --> 00:04:33.900
is the necessary toll for building

107
00:04:33.900 --> 00:04:36.940
a perfectly unified cross-platform pipeline.

108
00:04:36.940 --> 00:04:39.340
The architectural choice comes down to this,

109
00:04:39.340 --> 00:04:42.340
demanding total control over a cross-platform setup

110
00:04:42.340 --> 00:04:44.260
versus accepting graphical simplicity

111
00:04:44.260 --> 00:04:46.580
and letting Apple handle the details.

112
00:04:46.580 --> 00:04:49.020
This comparison scorecard grades both pipelines

113
00:04:49.020 --> 00:04:50.940
across three distinct team archetypes

114
00:04:50.940 --> 00:04:53.340
to show exactly where each tool belongs.

115
00:04:53.340 --> 00:04:55.380
First is the cross-platform team,

116
00:04:55.380 --> 00:04:57.460
operating a mono repo belt on frameworks

117
00:04:57.460 --> 00:04:59.260
like React Native or Flutter.

118
00:04:59.260 --> 00:05:02.340
For this group, GitHub actions is the definitive choice.

119
00:05:02.340 --> 00:05:04.780
Excode Cloud offers zero Linux support,

120
00:05:04.780 --> 00:05:07.420
making an Android build completely impossible.

121
00:05:07.420 --> 00:05:10.220
The upfront pane of configuring match and tuning caches

122
00:05:10.220 --> 00:05:12.540
is entirely outweighed by the long-term velocity

123
00:05:12.540 --> 00:05:15.460
of running one parallel pipeline for both platforms.

124
00:05:15.460 --> 00:05:18.820
The second archetype is the Apple exclusive team,

125
00:05:18.820 --> 00:05:23.100
building native applications strictly in Swift or SwiftUI.

126
00:05:23.100 --> 00:05:25.540
If your code base is purely native,

127
00:05:25.540 --> 00:05:28.980
avoid the complexity of GitHub actions entirely.

128
00:05:28.980 --> 00:05:31.540
Excode Cloud eliminates runner management,

129
00:05:31.540 --> 00:05:36.140
handles code signing and gives 25 free compute hours monthly.

130
00:05:36.140 --> 00:05:39.020
The final archetype is the budget optimizer.

131
00:05:39.020 --> 00:05:41.500
If using GitHub actions but lacking funds

132
00:05:41.500 --> 00:05:45.740
for inefficient macOS minutes, your setup must be flawless.

133
00:05:45.740 --> 00:05:47.780
You must pin your Excode versions,

134
00:05:47.780 --> 00:05:50.020
isolate the CocoaPod spec cache,

135
00:05:50.020 --> 00:05:51.940
and use manual certificate secrets

136
00:05:51.940 --> 00:05:55.660
to bypass dedicated fast lane repository overhead.

137
00:05:55.660 --> 00:05:58.580
There is no single best CI pipeline.

138
00:05:58.580 --> 00:06:00.660
The right choice is dictated entirely

139
00:06:00.660 --> 00:06:03.380
by whether your code base lives strictly inside Apple's

140
00:06:03.380 --> 00:06:06.740
walled garden or if it has to survive outside of it.

